1
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,580
CONVERSATION 1
Have you decided what you wish to study?

2
00:00:39,580 --> 00:00:40,660
Yes, professor.

3
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:43,000
I wish to study architecture.

4
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,500
Ah, that's a wonderful profession.

5
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:46,940
Why did you choose it?

6
00:00:46,940 --> 00:00:50,360
I gave a lot of thought to things you said during our last discussion.

7
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:54,180
And I decided architecture made the most sense for me.

8
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:59,360
In particular, I'm attracted to the marriage of design and engineering.

9
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:05,480
That's great, but I seem to remember you were preoccupied with all the lofty mathematics.

10
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:08,620
You no longer think this aspect will be too hard for you?

11
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:14,400
No. I was doing some online research and I think I should be able to manage.

12
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:20,940
Math might be the toughest subject I encounter in the curriculum, but I'll simply put in the work required.

13
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:23,420
I'm certain the fruits of my labor will pay off.

14
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:25,880
I'm sure that's true.

15
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:34,000
Due to its material nature, architecture is one field where you will be able to clearly witness the contribution you make to a city.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,540
And the legacies you build will last much longer than any of us mortals.

17
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:41,020
Yeah, I guess that's true too.

18
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:45,620
So what subjects do you think I should start brushing up on in preparation?

19
00:01:45,620 --> 00:01:46,780
I have the whole summer.

20
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:52,640
These days, architecture is a very diverse and dynamic degree.

21
00:01:52,640 --> 00:02:00,660
Most universities will encourage you to learn different things from fields as wide-ranging as philosophy and economics.

22
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:10,700
Nevertheless, I would suggest you start with computer science, because there will be many intricate visuals you will have to produce as part of your projects.

23
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:14,120
Any software in particular you think I should start with?

24
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:15,420
Photoshop is a good place.

25
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:21,080
If you aren't yet, try to become as proficient as you can with it.

26
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:37,380
And another thing that I would recommend you learn as much as you can is fine arts, in particular, the history of Western art.

27
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:55,660
Q1. Why does the woman wish to study architecture?

28
00:02:55,660 --> 00:03:15,340
Q2. How does the woman say she will overcome the difficulty in learning mathematics?

29
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:34,960
Q3. What does the man say about architects' contribution to a city?

30
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:54,240
Q4. What subject does the man suggest the woman start with?

31
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:59,020
CONVERSATION 2
So, as I mentioned over the phone, we are looking for someone who is very discreet.

32
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:00,740
I can't stress this enough.

33
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:02,700
I fully understand.

34
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:10,260
As we all know, being such a famous football player at Real Madrid, Mr Sanchez has a very high public profile.

35
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:14,960
This means he cannot leave the house without being recognized and harassed.

36
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:22,280
Yes, I completely understand how imperative it would be to respect Mr Sanchez's private life.

37
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:22,940
Good.

38
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:34,620
As his personal assistant, you would be required to run common everyday chores for Mr Sanchez, things he cannot do himself due to his fame, like going to the supermarket or post office.

39
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:40,500
So, well, at first sight it may appear that working for a celebrity is very glamorous.

40
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:42,760
Nothing could be further from the truth.

41
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:48,320
I'm telling you this so that you don't get any false expectations of what the job has to offer you.

42
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:49,100
Thanks.

43
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:50,820
I understand this too.

44
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:53,780
This is my first job after graduating from university.

45
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:58,540
So frankly, I don't have much prior working experience.

46
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:08,120
I am, however, very excited about the prospect of joining this company and very keen to start from the bottom in whatever capacity I may be of use.

47
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:09,240
That's good to hear.

48
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:11,000
You sound very energetic.

49
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,140
Tell me why do you think you are well suited for this job?

50
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:17,100
Well, firstly, I love football.

51
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:22,740
I'm a lifelong Real Madrid supporter and go to their games every weekend I can.

52
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:31,660
I would simply love to have a job where I'm involved in some capacity with Real Madrid, regardless of the position and salary.

53
00:05:31,660 --> 00:05:37,420
And secondly, I think what this company does in sports consultancy is very cool.

54
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:42,700
And I think my language skills could prove very useful down the road.

55
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:45,160
Please tell me more about your language skills.

56
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:58,080
I am fully fluent in English and Spanish and have a working proficiency in French.

57
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:16,540
Q5. What do we learn from the conversation about Mr Sanchez?

58
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:34,100
Q6. What will the man do if he gets the job?

59
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:51,260
Q7. What does the man say about himself?

60
00:06:51,260 --> 00:07:35,820
Q8. Why does the man think he is a very competitive candidate?

61
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:43,420
PASSAGE 1
Adventure sports are undeniably attractive to many energetic and thrill-seeking kids.

62
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:50,940
Sports like rock climbing, surfing, skiing, and mountain biking, thus have lots of appeal.

63
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:59,500
They don't have many of the rules and pressures of traditional team sports, but they still have all the benefits of outdoor physical activity.

64
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:06,680
When properly supervised, extreme sports can be a part of a healthy, balanced life.

65
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:17,220
Some adventure sports may be associated with higher risks of injury, so it's important for kids to figure out when to take risks, and when not to.

66
00:08:17,220 --> 00:08:23,480
Risk-free activities deprive kids of the opportunity to test themselves and overcome their fears.

67
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:30,640
But, to some, these very risks are what makes these sports a bad choice.

68
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:41,620
They say that extreme sports can often lead to devastating injuries, especially as young thrill seekers may attempt feats that are too advanced for them.

69
00:08:41,620 --> 00:08:43,200
But let's be real.

70
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:48,720
Most kids do all kinds of dangerous stuff whether you want them to or not.

71
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:51,960
Adventure sports have many of the same risks and dangers.

72
00:08:51,960 --> 00:09:00,860
But, they have an additional sense of adventure, courage and autonomy that is important to foster in young children.

73
00:09:00,860 --> 00:09:05,460
As always, it's up to parents to decide what's best for their kids.

74
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:15,560
But, if you do decide that adventure sports are a bit too much, do try to introduce other forms of outdoor adventures into their lives.

75
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:40,886
Camping under the stars, fishing, stargazing, or even just playing in the woods can help a lot in building the same sense of self-confidence and risk-taking as in adventure sports.

76
00:09:40,886 --> 00:09:54,340
Q9. Why do extreme sports appeal to many kids?

77
00:09:54,340 --> 00:10:12,320
Q10. What does the speaker say about risk-free activities?

78
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:33,680
Q11. What shall parents do if they decide adventure sports are a bit too much for their kids?

79
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:38,240
PASSAGE 2
The shiny gadgets of today will be waste tomorrow.

80
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:47,740
Manufacturers don't talk much about this when they announce the big new thing that will replace your mostly just as good old thing.

81
00:10:47,740 --> 00:11:00,340
In fact, technology firms often purposely design devices that will not last long and cannot be repaired so that consumers will have to spend their money on a new one.

82
00:11:00,340 --> 00:11:09,240
This year, the French government began requiring tech manufacturers to list a repairability score.

83
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:20,560
If a device can be repaired, then its life can be extended, saving consumers money and the planet the burden of so many trashed gadgets.

84
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:26,240
Equipped with this knowledge, consumers can make better choices about which products to buy.

85
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:39,800
Some 59 million tons of old TVs, computers, screens, smartphones, washers and other electronics are discarded every year.

86
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:42,200
This waste is dangerous.

87
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:46,140
Batteries explode in recycling facilities.

88
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:53,140
Toxic substances like mercury leak into soil and groundwater and disperse in the air.

89
00:11:53,140 --> 00:11:59,120
Manufacturing flat screens adds greenhouse gases to the atmosphere.

90
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:03,940
We need tech companies to take the lead to solve this problem.

91
00:12:03,940 --> 00:12:08,200
If they won't, governments must make them do so.

92
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:20,400
Although tech companies will often speak of environmental sustainability, many lobby against repair legislation, fearful it will eat into their profits.

93
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:27,840
Sustainability matters, but marketable design appears to matter more to these companies.

94
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:31,600
Consumers should support right-to-repair legislation.

95
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:35,020
In this world, damage is a certainty.

96
00:12:35,020 --> 00:12:48,960
But we cannot leave things broken: A problem of our creation is a problem that can be fixed.

97
00:12:48,960 --> 00:13:07,460
Q12. Why did new tech products soon become waste?

98
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:29,060
Q13. What did the French government require tech companies to do to help consumers make better choices?

99
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:49,340
Q14. What should tech companies do to help ensure environmental sustainability?

100
00:13:49,340 --> 00:14:33,750
Q15. What does the speaker think of the problem of waste?

101
00:14:33,750 --> 00:14:44,060
RECORDING 1
On average, U.S. workers spend about 10% of their workday surfing the internet, emailing friends or shopping online.

102
00:14:44,060 --> 00:14:54,300
This behavior, called cyberloafing, is believed to cost employers up to $85 billion a year in lost productivity.

103
00:14:54,300 --> 00:15:08,200
The majority of cyberloafing research focuses on ways to prevent employees from engaging in this behavior through interventions such as internet monitoring and computer use policies.

104
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:15,920
But it turns out, such behaviors may not be a sign that a worker is lazy or just wasting time.

105
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:23,880
Social media use at work has been linked to higher levels of employee engagement and job satisfaction.

106
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:31,440
New research suggests cyberloafing can help workers cope with an exceptionally stressful work environment.

107
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:39,020
But is cyberloafing actually effective at reducing employee stress levels?

108
00:15:39,020 --> 00:15:43,780
That's the question my research team wanted to answer in our new study.

109
00:15:43,780 --> 00:15:54,500
Our hypothesis was that cyberloafing may serve as a mini-break, giving employees an opportunity to recover from stressful work situations.

110
00:15:54,500 --> 00:16:06,340
To test this, we asked workers to complete an online survey and rank how much time they spent cyberloafing, checking non-work emails and shopping.

111
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:19,340
We also asked them to rank job satisfaction, their desire to quit, and how often they've experienced mistreatment at work, such as being bullied, threatened or yelled at.

112
00:16:19,340 --> 00:16:33,080
As you might expect, we found that overall, more mistreatment at work was correlated with lower levels of job satisfaction and a greater desire to quit.

113
00:16:33,080 --> 00:16:40,240
More interestingly, we found that cyberloafing effectively buffered this connection.

114
00:16:40,240 --> 00:16:56,920
That is, mistreated workers who spent more time surfing the web and checking emails reported higher job satisfaction and were less likely to want to quit than similar participants who didn't cyberloaf as much.

115
00:16:56,920 --> 00:17:07,500
This suggests that cyberloafing acts as a sort of relief valve for workers, helping them recover from stressful experiences.

116
00:17:07,500 --> 00:17:22,340
While we did not directly assess how cyberloafing affects worker performance, we believe by relieving stress it may not only reduce worker turnover, but also ultimately bolster productivity.

117
00:17:22,340 --> 00:17:31,120
This fits with other recent research that suggests taking short breaks is associated with higher levels of daily job performance.

118
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:35,080
That isn't to say cyberloafing is always good.

119
00:17:35,080 --> 00:17:40,660
Too much time spent on non-work activities likely causes performance to suffer.

120
00:17:40,660 --> 00:17:46,660
All in all, managers should be more lenient with employees.

121
00:17:46,660 --> 00:18:06,263
We believe a bit of online shopping or surfing the internet at work could make workers more productive in the long run.

122
00:18:06,263 --> 00:18:20,520
Q16. What does most cyberloafing research focus on?

123
00:18:20,520 --> 00:18:38,880
Q17. What was the hypothesis of the speaker's research team?

124
00:18:38,880 --> 00:18:59,820
Q18. On what point do the results of the speaker's study agree with other recent research?

125
00:18:59,820 --> 00:19:10,640
RECORDING 2
When Julius Morton moved to Nebraska City in 1854, he faced a problem shared by many settlers in the territory: It was a treeless plain.

126
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:15,820
That meant no trees for building materials, or for fuel.

127
00:19:15,820 --> 00:19:26,660
But Morton was one of the world's first conservationists, stating, "For prosperity, we ought to plant as many forests as we have exhausted and consumed".

128
00:19:26,660 --> 00:19:30,660
So he started planting trees, beginning with his own land.

129
00:19:30,660 --> 00:19:36,160
By 1860 Morton possessed a forest of more than 300 trees.

130
00:19:36,160 --> 00:19:39,640
A few years later, he had more than 1,000.

131
00:19:39,640 --> 00:19:44,480
As the forest grew, so did Morton's influence in Nebraska.

132
00:19:44,480 --> 00:19:57,720
This was largely because Morton founded the Nebraska City News, the state's first newspaper, in which he frequently wrote editorials about the practical and aesthetic benefits of tree planting.

133
00:19:57,720 --> 00:20:05,340
He also organized the Nebraska State Gardening Society and served on the Nebraska State Board of Agriculture.

134
00:20:05,340 --> 00:20:12,840
While serving on the Board, Morton came up with an idea to spread his belief in tree planting statewide.

135
00:20:12,840 --> 00:20:27,950
On January 4, 1872, Morton drafted a resolution that April 10 be designated a day for the planting of trees in the State of Nebraska and urged people to go out and plant trees themselves.

136
00:20:27,950 --> 00:20:33,630
Morton called the special event Arbor Day as "arbor" is Latin for "tree".

137
00:20:33,630 --> 00:20:35,410
The state government agreed.

138
00:20:35,410 --> 00:20:43,050
And on April 10, 1872, the first unofficial Arbor Day was celebrated throughout Nebraska.

139
00:20:43,050 --> 00:20:49,810
Prizes were awarded to counties, cities, and individuals who planted the largest number of trees.

140
00:20:49,810 --> 00:21:01,290
That day, an astounding one million trees were planted in Nebraska—an average of more than six for every man, woman and child in the state.

141
00:21:01,290 --> 00:21:11,390
Since 1885, Nebraska has planted more than 700,000 acres of trees, earning it the nickname "the Tree Planters State".

142
00:21:11,390 --> 00:21:18,350
Arbor Day became a legal, civic holiday in the state in 1885.

143
00:21:18,350 --> 00:21:23,210
It was held on April 22—Julius Morton's birthday.

144
00:21:23,210 --> 00:21:35,590
In addition to a parade in Nebraska City, Morton introduced what has since become a long-standing Arbor Day tradition: Schoolchildren went outside and planted trees together.

145
00:21:35,590 --> 00:21:48,290
In 1970, nearly a century after Arbor Day was first celebrated, President Richard Nixon declared the last Friday of every April to be observed as National Arbor Day.

146
00:21:48,290 --> 00:22:05,490
All 50 states recognize the April observance, although many hold an additional state Arbor Day in a month more suited to local tree planting.

147
00:22:05,490 --> 00:22:26,210
Q19. What does the speaker say about Nebraska City in 1854 when Julius Morton moved there?

148
00:22:26,210 --> 00:22:46,070
Q20. What did Julius Morton do that increased his influence in Nebraska City?

149
00:22:46,070 --> 00:23:06,290
Q21. What does the speaker say happened on April 10, 1872?

150
00:23:06,290 --> 00:23:11,190
RECORDING 3
Modern humans arose in Africa about 200,000 years ago.

151
00:23:11,190 --> 00:23:17,110
They then spread across Europe and Asia sometime after 60,000 years ago.

152
00:23:17,110 --> 00:23:21,870
This is the "Out of Africa" model, as it's commonly known.

153
00:23:21,870 --> 00:23:26,610
In the 1990s, the hypothesis found widespread acceptance.

154
00:23:26,610 --> 00:23:36,010
But this popular idea is in need of revision, particularly given the number of important findings across Asia over the past few decades.

155
00:23:36,010 --> 00:23:45,670
For instance, many new human fossils found, particularly in China, are now dated as older than 60,000 years.

156
00:23:45,670 --> 00:23:53,850
This calls into question the idea that modern humans migrated out of Africa only 60,000 years ago.

157
00:23:53,850 --> 00:24:02,950
Take the recent discovery of two modern human teeth found in the Luna cave in China's Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region.

158
00:24:02,950 --> 00:24:17,750
When my research team and I dated these teeth, we found they were more than 70,000 years old—a situation clearly impossible if modern humans moved out of Africa only 60,000 years ago.

159
00:24:17,750 --> 00:24:21,330
So with such findings, what happened exactly?

160
00:24:21,330 --> 00:24:24,930
Where does the most current data suggest we came from?

161
00:24:24,930 --> 00:24:31,610
The first question we should ask is why did modern humans leave Africa to begin with?

162
00:24:31,610 --> 00:24:43,790
If a population is perfectly adapted to a particular environment and has access to an abundance of resources, then there really is no reason to move or change.

163
00:24:43,790 --> 00:24:56,390
For instance, some monkeys in Africa have a set of teeth that has hardly changed over the course of millions of years, indicating that they found a place that has worked for them.

164
00:24:56,390 --> 00:24:58,550
So what happened with humans?

165
00:24:58,550 --> 00:25:07,690
Some researchers have suggested that population density increased to the point where smaller human groups were forced to explore new lands.

166
00:25:07,690 --> 00:25:16,730
Other researchers have suggested that due to major environmental events in East Africa, humans were prompted to find greener pastures.

167
00:25:16,730 --> 00:25:34,610
Yet another explanation could simply be that early modern human hunters were following the large animals that they relied on, and so they moved out of Africa without realizing that they were actually moving from one continent to another.

168
00:25:34,610 --> 00:25:41,870
Humans need daily access to reliable fresh water, which appears to be absent from many coastal areas.

169
00:25:41,870 --> 00:25:48,870
This brings us to another question: by which route did modern humans move out of Africa?

170
00:25:48,870 --> 00:26:11,890
No clear routes across the Mediterranean from North Africa appear to be present, so the earlier dispersals out of Africa and into Europe and Asia might have been across the Arabian Peninsula.

171
00:26:11,890 --> 00:26:33,390
Q22. What do we learn about modern humans according to the hypothesis commonly accepted in the 1990s?

172
00:26:33,390 --> 00:26:52,110
Q23. What has called into question the "Out of Africa" model?

173
00:26:52,110 --> 00:27:12,310
Q24. What does the speaker want to show with the example of some monkeys in Africa?

174
00:27:12,310 --> 00:27:32,767
Q25. What question does the speaker raise close to the end of the talk?
